#- Printing
#-
#- A value in Aurora is a tape: a fixed run of bytes. It is never a number, a
#- character or a string on its own — those are readings of the same tape, and
#- there is one builtin per reading:
#-
#-   printb   the bytes themselves
#-   printd   the number they spell, big-endian
#-   printc   the text those bytes are, in UTF-8
#-
#- Nothing is converted between them. The tape holding 44 is one tape; read as
#- bytes it is [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 44], as a number 44, as text a comma.
#-
#- Run: aurora run examples/printing.ar
#-
#- Output:
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 44]
#-   44
#-   ,
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 104 105]
#-   26729
#-   hi
#-   1
#-   [0 0 0 99 97 102 195 169]
#-   café
#-   [0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0]
#-   0

#- One number, three readings.
printb 44;
printd 44;
printc 44;

#- printc reads the bytes as UTF-8, so a number with more than one byte spells
#- more than one character: 26729 is the bytes 104 and 105, which are h and i.
printb 26729;
printd 26729;
printc 26729;

#- And "hi" is that same tape written another way, so it answers the same to all
#- three. Text is not a kind of value: it is how those bytes were typed.
printd "hi" equals 26729;

#- The bytes are UTF-8, which is what keeps an accent whole: é is two bytes.
printb "café";
printc "café";

#- A tape of zeros is the neutral value. It spells zero and names no character,
#- so printc has nothing to write and writes an empty line.
printb false;
printd false;
printc false;
